The sysstat package installs its files across several standard directories on Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL). The main user executables (like sar, iostat, and mpstat) are installed in /usr/bin/.

Here is a breakdown of the primary directories where sysstat components are placed:

1. User Executables (/usr/bin/)

This is where the tools you run manually in the terminal are located:
• /usr/bin/sar (System activity reporter)
• /usr/bin/iostat (CPU and I/O statistics)
• /usr/bin/mpstat (Multiprocessor statistics)
• /usr/bin/pidstat (Process statistics)
• /usr/bin/tapestat (Tape drive statistics)
• /usr/bin/cifsiostat (CIFS I/O statistics)

these are binary files which are actually executing and getting the report
you can use commands like : sar iostat  mpstat  pidstat tapestat    cifsiostat


2. Internal Collection Tools (/usr/lib64/sa/ or /usr/lib/sa/)

The background engines that collect data are separated from the main user tools:
• sa1: A binary that collects and stores binary data in the daily data file.
• sa2: A shell script that writes a daily report.
• sadc: The system activity data collector backend.

3. Systemd Services and Timers (/usr/lib/systemd/system/)

On modern RHEL versions (RHEL 8, 9, and 10), background collection is managed by systemd:
• /usr/lib/systemd/system/sysstat.service (Initializes collection on boot)
• /usr/lib/systemd/system/sysstat-collect.timer (Triggers data collection every 10 minutes)
• /usr/lib/systemd/system/sysstat-summary.timer (Triggers daily summary generation)


